7 Permissions
*************

Each tool belongs to a class: ‘read’, ‘edit’, ‘shell’, or ‘network’.  A
read never asks.  An edit asks.  A shell asks.  A network call asks.

   A ‘run_shell’ command that Harmless can see is read-only is treated
as a read, so it does not ask.  A command Harmless does not recognize
still asks.  A redirect that writes, a command substitution, and a
subshell still ask.  ‘shell_kill’ still asks.  In plan mode a read-only
shell command still runs, and a shell command that can write is refused
before the question.  *Note Plan mode::.  A file watch does not ask.  A
command that can write asks, and the question shows that command.  *Note
Monitors::.  Scheduling a prompt asks, and the question shows that
prompt.  Accepting edits does not ask.  *Note Schedules::.

   The permission mode of a session changes that.

‘ask’
     The default.  Edits, shell commands, and network calls ask.

‘accept-edits’
     Edits proceed.  Shell commands and network calls still ask.

‘always-approve’
     Edits, shell commands, and network calls proceed.

‘auto’
     A reviewer decides an escalation.  A call that would ask is checked
     first.  ‘allow’ skips the question.  ‘escalate’ asks.  An allow
     rule skips the reviewer.  A deny, a dangerous command, and a shell
     write in plan mode still refuse before the reviewer.  ‘ask’,
     ‘accept-edits’, and ‘always-approve’ do not call the reviewer.  A
     read still does not ask.

   ‘M-x harmless-set-permission-mode’ sets the mode for the current
session.  The menu key is ‘p’.  New sessions take the value of
‘harmless-permission-mode’, which defaults to ‘ask’.

   The shell sandbox is separate from the permission mode.  It defaults
to ‘off’.  ‘read-only’ and ‘workspace-write’ use Landlock on Linux.
‘M-x harmless-set-shell-sandbox’ sets it for the current session.  The
menu key is ‘S’.  New sessions take ‘harmless-shell-sandbox’, which
defaults to ‘off’.  Nil, ‘""’, and an unknown name are refused: ‘shell
sandbox must be off, read-only, or workspace-write’.  A saved value
Harmless does not know is loaded as ‘off’.  ‘off’ is not written into
the session summary.

   ‘run_shell’ takes ‘network’.  It is a boolean.  Omit it, or pass
false, and a sandbox that is on denies new network connections.  Pass
true and the call asks as a network tool before it runs.  A value that
is not a boolean is refused when the sandbox is on: ‘Error: network must
be a boolean’.  When the sandbox is ‘off’, ‘network’ is ignored and a
read-only command still does not ask.  A kernel that cannot restrict the
network is refused rather than left open: ‘Error: Landlock cannot
restrict the network on this kernel’.

   A read-only subagent keeps a read-only Landlock sandbox when the
session sandbox is not ‘off’.  When the session sandbox is ‘off’, that
subagent is not sandboxed.  The subagent's own ‘sandbox’ field still
chooses which tools it may call.  *Note Subagents::.  *Note Shell::.

   When a tool needs a decision, the prompt shows the class and a short
description of the call.  For an edit, that description is the path.
For a shell command, it is the command.  For a web search, it is the
query.  For a fetched page, it is the URL. For an X search, it is the
query or the post id.  For an MCP call, it is the resource uri or the
server name.  For a click, it is the point.  For typed text, it is the
text.  For a key, it is the key.  For a browser, it is the URL. A
screenshot shows the tool name.  A file watch does not ask.  A command
that can write shows the command.  A scheduled prompt shows the prompt.
Cancelling one shows the id.

‘y’
     Allow this call.

‘n’
     Deny this call.  The model receives an error and can try something
     else.

‘!’
     Allow this class for the rest of the session.

   Those three keys insert themselves when no question is pending, so
you can type them in an ordinary prompt.

   A denied call does not change the project.  An allowed edit or shell
command runs at once.

   ‘harmless-permission-rules’ and ‘harmless-permission-denies’ are
lists of rules copied onto a session when it is created and when it is
loaded.  A rule set only on that session is not saved, and loading the
session replaces it with these lists.

   ‘.harmless/permissions’ in the project is an allow list that survives
the session.  One rule per line.  A blank line is ignored.  A line that
starts with ‘#’ is a comment.  Those rules are copied onto every session
in that project, including one that is loaded again.  A detached chat
has no project file.  Nil, ‘""’, a relative path, and the filesystem
root are not a project.  A missing file adds no rules and creates
nothing.  A directory at that path is refused: ‘permissions file is a
directory’.  A bad line is refused with the file path, a colon, and the
rule error.  The file is not copied into the session, and saving the
session does not write it.

   A rule is ‘Bash(PATTERN)’, ‘Edit(PATTERN)’, or
‘WebFetch(domain:HOST)’.  ‘Bash(npm run *)’ matches a shell command, and
the same form matches a command monitor.  ‘*’ matches any characters,
including spaces and slashes.  A file watch is not a command.  ‘/’ and
‘c:/’ are command patterns, not paths.

   ‘Edit(/src/**)’ matches a path inside the project.  ‘*’ matches one
path segment.  ‘**’ matches across segments.  A leading slash is the
project, not the filesystem root.  ‘/’, ‘c:/’, a drive prefix, ‘..’, and
‘~’ are refused when the rule is read.  ‘WebFetch(domain:example.com)’
matches that host and a subdomain.  It does not match a search.

   A deny is checked before the question.  It wins over an allow, and it
still refuses in ‘accept-edits’ and ‘always-approve’, including a read
that would not have asked.  The model receives ‘Error: Permission rule
denied ’, followed by the rule.  An allow skips the question when every
command, path, or host matches.  A patch is allowed only when every hunk
path matches.  One denied hunk refuses the whole patch, and nothing is
written.  Plan mode still refuses a shell command that can write, before
these rules.  A read-only command that plan mode allows can still be
denied.

   ‘harmless-dangerous-commands’ names programs that are refused before
the question, including in ‘always-approve’.  An allow rule does not
override the list.  The match is the lower-case file name.  ‘sudo’ and
‘doas’ are unwrapped once.  A name that starts with ‘mkfs.’ is refused.
‘rm’ of ‘/’, a drive root, ‘~’, or ‘/*’ is refused too.  A longer path
is not.  The model receives ‘Error: dangerous command refused: ’,
followed by the program name.  In plan mode a command that can write is
still refused as a plan-mode write.

   In ‘auto’ mode the reviewer runs only when the call would ask and no
allow rule matches.  ‘harmless-auto-review-function’, when set, is that
classifier.  It is called with the session, the tool, and the arguments,
and it returns ‘allow’ or ‘escalate’.  Any other return, or an error,
escalates.  Nil asks the reviewer model.  The model sees the tool name
and the arguments and is given no tools.  Only a reply that is exactly
‘allow’ or ‘escalate’ is kept.  A missing provider, an error, a tool
call, or any other reply escalates.  The reviewer does not run the tool.
The classifier is not saved on the session.  The mode is saved.  ‘M-x
harmless-set-permission-mode’ accepts ‘auto’.  Nil uses the default
mode.  An unknown mode is refused: ‘permission mode is not a mode’.  A
saved mode Harmless does not know is loaded as ‘ask’.

   ‘harmless-hooks’ is a list of hooks.  It is not saved on the session.
A ‘pre’ hook can block a tool call before the question.  The model
receives ‘Error: hook blocked this tool call’, or that text, a colon,
and the reason.  A block does not run the tool.  A hook cannot override
plan mode, the dangerous-command list, or a deny rule, and a hook cannot
skip the question.  A ‘post’ hook runs after the tool and can replace
the result the model sees.  The files the tool wrote stay as they are.
A hook error blocks before the call and leaves the result it was given
after the call.  A hook is a function, a command, an HTTP request, an
MCP call, a prompt, or a subagent.  Nil, ‘""’, ‘/’, and ‘c:/’ are not
URLs and are not fetched.


