10.3 Shell
==========

‘run_shell’ runs one command in the project root through your shell.
The next command starts in the same directory when a ‘cd’ stayed inside
the project.  Nil and ‘""’ mean the project root.  A ‘cd’ that leaves
the project is not kept.  Harmless reports ‘The shell left the project.
The next command starts in ’, followed by the directory the next command
starts in.  A remembered directory is used only when it is still inside
the project.  The filesystem root is not kept.  The remembered directory
is saved with the session.

   ‘timeout’ is how many seconds to wait.  When it is omitted,
‘harmless-shell-timeout-seconds’ (120) is used.  A negative number,
zero, or a value that is not a number is refused: ‘Error: timeout must
be a positive number’.  An empty command is refused: ‘Error: command is
empty’.  When the command is still running at the end of that wait,
Harmless leaves the process running and returns a background id with the
output captured so far.  It does not kill the process.  ‘background’
true returns that id immediately.

   ‘shell_output’ reads one background shell.  ‘id’ is the id
‘run_shell’ returned.  Without ‘timeout’, the tool returns the output
already available.  With ‘timeout’, it waits that many seconds for the
command to exit, then returns whatever it has.  This tool does not ask
for permission.  An empty id is refused: ‘Error: shell id is empty’.  An
unknown id is refused: ‘Error: no background shell ’, followed by that
id.  A bad timeout uses the same refusal as ‘run_shell’.

   ‘shell_kill’ stops a background shell.  It asks for permission like
any other shell command.  Killing a shell that is not running is
refused: ‘Error: shell ’, the id, and ‘ is not running’.

   The text returned to the model is cut at
‘harmless-shell-output-max-bytes’ (20000).

   Abort stops a shell that is still in the foreground.  A shell that
has moved to the background keeps running.  When it exits, or when it is
killed, Emacs shows a message and a note in the transcript.  That note
is not a message the model sees on the next turn.  The jobs are kept in
memory for this Emacs session.  They are not written to the session
files.

   A command Harmless recognizes as read-only does not ask for
permission.  A command it does not recognize still asks.  A redirect
that writes, a command substitution, and a subshell ask.  *Note
Permissions::.

   In plan mode, a shell command that can write is refused before any
permission question: ‘Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that can
write is refused’.  Nil, ‘""’, and a blank command are not that refusal.
A read-only command still runs.  ‘shell_kill’ still asks.  *Note Plan
mode::.

   When ‘harmless-shell-sandbox’ is ‘off’, the default, the shell is not
sandboxed and ‘network’ is ignored.  The process is your shell.  It is
not the Landlock helper.

   ‘read-only’ and ‘workspace-write’ run that command through
‘harmless-landlock’ on Linux.  The program is the one beside the Lisp
sources, or ‘harmless-landlock-program’ when that is an absolute
executable.  The shell can read and execute from the filesystem root.
‘read-only’ adds no write rule.  A redirect into the project, into
‘/tmp’, or into ‘/dev/null’ fails, and the file is not created.

   ‘workspace-write’ can write the project, each directory added for the
session, ‘temporary-file-directory’, ‘TMPDIR’ when that is an absolute
directory, ‘/tmp’, ‘/var/tmp’, and ‘/dev/shm’ when those exist and are
not the filesystem root.  It can also write ‘/dev/null’, ‘/dev/zero’,
‘/dev/full’, ‘/dev/tty’, ‘/dev/urandom’, and ‘/dev/random’ when they
exist and their real path stays under ‘/dev’.  It does not receive a
write rule for the filesystem root.  The Harmless data directory is not
a write root of its own, nor is a directory inside it, unless that path
is the project or strictly inside the project.  A data directory inside
the project stays writable, because a write rule cannot carve a hole
inside the project.  A directory outside those roots is not created.
Changing directory is not a write.  A ‘cd’ that leaves the project is
still not remembered.

   ‘network’ true asks for permission and then allows network
connections.  Omit it, or pass false, and new TCP connections are
denied.  UDP is denied when the kernel can restrict it.  An empty
command is still ‘Error: command is empty’, before the sandbox.  In plan
mode a command that can write is still refused before the sandbox.

   On a system that is not Linux the sandbox is refused: ‘Error:
Landlock sandbox requires Linux’.  A missing helper, a relative path, or
a program that is not executable is refused: ‘Error: Landlock helper is
missing’.  A kernel without Landlock is refused: ‘Error: Landlock is not
available’.  A path the helper cannot open is refused: ‘Error: Landlock
could not open ’, followed by that path.  A relative sandbox path is
refused: ‘Error: sandbox path must be absolute’.  A write of the
filesystem root is refused: ‘Error: refusing to sandbox-write the
filesystem root’.  A helper that cannot restrict the process reports
‘Error: Landlock could not restrict this process’.  A command the helper
cannot run reports ‘Error: Landlock could not run’, followed by the
program when it has one.  *Note Permissions::.


