11 Instructions
***************

Harmless reads these names in each directory, from your home directory
down to the session directory:

  1. ‘CLAUDE.md’, when ‘harmless-claude-md’ says to read it

  2. ‘AGENTS.md’

  3. ‘HARMLESS.md’

  4. ‘.harmless/HARMLESS.md’

   ‘harmless-claude-md’ has three values.  ‘nil’ never reads
‘CLAUDE.md’.  ‘t’ always reads it.  ‘anthropic’, the default, reads it
only when the session model id starts with ‘claude-’.  A model such as
‘grok-4.6’ does not match.  ‘CLAUDE.md’ is read before the other three
names, so those later files win when they disagree.

   A file applies to its directory and everything under it.  Outer
directories come first.  At one directory, the files are read in the
order above.  When two files disagree, the later one wins.  Empty files
are skipped.  The walk stops at your home directory and at the
filesystem root.

   An instruction file can include another file by writing ‘@path’.  The
path is relative to the file that contains the import, not the session
directory.  A backslash before a space is part of the path.  The file's
text replaces the import.  An import inside inline code or a fenced code
block is left as written.  A quoted path is left as written.  The file
must stay inside the session directory.  Harmless refuses an empty path,
a missing file, a directory, a cycle, and a path that leaves the
session.  The filesystem root is not a directory an import may enter.

   The text is sent with every turn.  It is not copied into the
transcript.  A detached chat does not walk ‘default-directory’ looking
for these files.

Path-scoped rules
=================

Harmless also reads Markdown files under ‘.claude/rules/’ in the session
directory.  Files in subdirectories are included.  The subdirectory name
does not choose which project files the rule applies to.  A file with no
‘paths’ field in its YAML frontmatter is sent on every turn.  A file
with ‘paths’ is sent only when a tool call in the transcript names a
file that matches one of those globs.  ‘*’ matches inside one path
segment.  ‘**’ matches across segments, including none.  A rule in a
parent directory is not read.  Nil, an empty string, a relative path,
and the filesystem root are not searched.

   The rule text is added to the system message for that turn.  It is
not copied into the transcript.  When two rules disagree, the later file
in sorted path order wins.

Standing prompt
===============

Every turn starts with a standing harness prompt.  It states which tool
to use, when to verify, and when to delegate.  A project chat and a
detached chat both receive it.  Project instructions, path-scoped rules,
and, in plan mode, the plan rules are sent after it.  When they disagree
with the harness prompt, they win.  The prompt is not copied into the
transcript.  Nil, an empty string, and a relative directory still
receive the prompt, and they still do not walk ‘default-directory’.


File: harmless.info,  Node: Skills,  Next: Plugins,  Prev: Instructions,  Up: Top

12 Skills
*********

A skill is a directory that contains ‘SKILL.md’.  The file starts with
‘name’ and ‘description’ in YAML frontmatter.  If the name is missing,
Harmless uses the directory name.  If the description is missing, it
uses the first paragraph of the body.

   Each turn receives a catalog: the name, the description, and the
path.  The body stays out of that catalog.  The model can still open the
file with ‘read_file’.

   Load one skill with the ‘skill’ tool, with ‘M-x harmless-load-skill’,
or by starting a prompt with ‘/name’.  The name is a skill id, not a
path.  Later turns include the body.  The prompt that loaded it stays in
the transcript.  A detached chat cannot load a skill, and it is not
offered the tool.

   ‘allowed-tools’ in the frontmatter limits the tools on those turns.
A missing field leaves the tools unchanged.  An empty list offers no
tools.  A tool name Harmless does not have is refused, and the skill
that was already loaded stays loaded.  A read-only subagent still cannot
write, even when the list names a writing tool.

   Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘skill name is empty’.  A
value that is not text is refused: ‘skill name must be a string’.  A
path, ‘~’, and the filesystem root are refused: ‘skill name is not a
name’.  A missing skill is refused: ‘no skill named ’, followed by the
id.  A relative project directory, including ‘~’, is refused and is not
expanded: ‘skill directory must be absolute’.  ‘/’ and a drive root are
refused: ‘skill directory is the filesystem root’.

   Harmless looks for a ‘skills’ directory inside these vendor
directories, from your home directory down to the session:

‘.agents/skills’
‘.codex/skills’
     Codex.

‘.claude/skills’
     Claude Code.

‘.grok/skills’
     Grok Build.

‘.harmless/skills’
     Harmless.

   In one directory, a later entry in that list replaces an earlier
skill of the same name.  A skill closer to the session replaces one of
the same name farther away.  ‘/etc/codex/skills’ is included when it
exists, at the lowest priority.  Skills that ship inside the Codex
application, and are not files on disk, are not read.

   A working directory can declare the skills it wants.  The file is
‘.harmless/loadout’, one skill id per line.  Blank lines are skipped.  A
comment, a path, a duplicate, and any other non-name are refused: ‘skill
loadout is not valid’.  A missing file is refused: ‘skill loadout is
missing’.  A file with no names is refused: ‘skill loadout is empty’.

   ‘M-x harmless-skill-loadout’ reconciles that file against skills
discovered in that directory only.  The vendor ‘skills’ folders in that
directory are read first.  Installed plugins in that directory come
after them, so a plugin skill wins on a clash.  Parent directories,
‘/etc/codex/skills’, and plugins installed for the user are not
included.  The result lists the declared names that are present, the
declared names that are missing, and the discovered names that were not
declared.

   A prefix argument writes ‘.harmless/loadout.managed’ with the present
names, one per line.  When none of the declared names were found, that
file is empty.  Without a prefix argument, nothing is written.  The
command does not copy a skill and it does not install a plugin.

   Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘skill loadout directory is
empty’.  A value that is not text is refused: ‘skill loadout directory
must be a string’.  A relative path, including ‘~’, is refused and is
not expanded: ‘skill loadout directory must be absolute’.  ‘/’ and a
drive root are refused: ‘skill loadout directory is the filesystem
root’.  A missing directory is refused: ‘skill loadout directory is
missing’.  A file is refused: ‘skill loadout directory is not a
directory’.  A trailing slash names the same directory.  Anything other
than nil or t for the apply flag is refused: ‘skill loadout apply is not
a flag’.


