14 Output styles
****************

An output style is a role, tone, and format for one session.  ‘M-x
harmless-output-style’ switches it.  Switching does not edit
‘AGENTS.md’, ‘HARMLESS.md’, ‘.harmless/HARMLESS.md’, or ‘CLAUDE.md’.

   A style file is markdown.  Optional frontmatter holds ‘name’ and
‘description’.  The body is the text later turns send.  The name is a
style id, not a path.  ‘default’ is not a style.  Nil, ‘""’, and
‘default’ clear the current style.  Whitespace is refused: ‘output style
name is empty’.  A value that is not text is refused: ‘output style name
must be a string’.  A path, ‘~’, and the filesystem root are refused:
‘output style name is not a name’.  A missing style is refused: ‘no
output style named ’, followed by the id.  An empty body is refused:
‘output style body is empty’.  The previous style stays in place when a
switch is refused.

   User styles are ‘output-styles’ under the Harmless data directory,
then ‘~/.claude/output-styles’.  Project styles are
‘.claude/output-styles’ and ‘.harmless/output-styles’ under the session
directory only.  Ancestors are not scanned.  A project style wins over a
user style.  In the project, ‘.harmless’ wins over ‘.claude’.  A
detached chat sees user styles only.

   A relative project directory, including ‘~’, is refused and is not
expanded: ‘output style directory must be absolute’.  ‘/’ and a drive
root are refused: ‘output style directory is the filesystem root’.  A
blank project directory is refused: ‘This chat is not attached to a
project’.

   The chosen name and body are saved with the session.  Loading the
session restores them.  A fork keeps the style it was given.  The
harness prompt stays first.  The style comes next.  Project
instructions, path-scoped rules, and plan mode win when they disagree.


