18 Team
*******

A rig is a named team.  A pod is a named group of seats.  A seat has a
name, a role, and an address.  The address is ‘pod-member@rig’, for
example ‘dev-build@workshop’.  The session that occupies the seat can be
replaced.  The seat keeps its name, its role, and its inbound work.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-add-seat’ records the seat.  It starts with no
session and no work.  ‘M-x harmless-team-occupy’ sets the session id.
Calling it again replaces that session.  ‘M-x harmless-team-add-work’
appends one text to the inbound work.  ‘M-x harmless-team-seat’ reads
the seat back.

   The record is ‘teams/RIG.json’ under ‘harmless-directory’.  *Note
Files::.  A pod name and a seat name are letters and digits.  A rig name
may also contain hyphens.  The address uses the stored spelling.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-edge’ records one edge.  The ends are ‘pod.member’
or the address.  ‘delegates_to’ starts the source before the target.
‘spawned_by’ starts the target before the source.  ‘can_observe’,
‘collaborates_with’, and ‘escalates_to’ are recorded and shown, and they
do not change the order.  ‘M-x harmless-team-edges’ reads the edges.
‘M-x harmless-team-launch-order’ reads the order.  It does not start a
seat.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-preview’ shows the seats, the edges, and the
launch order a boot would record.  It does not write.  The spec is the
name ‘starter’, an absolute JSON file, or a directory that contains
‘rig.json’.  ‘starter’ records two seats and one ‘delegates_to’ edge.
‘M-x harmless-team-boot’ records that rig.  Booting the same rig again
is refused: ‘team rig already exists’.  ‘M-x harmless-team-grow’ adds a
seat to a rig that is already recorded.  ‘M-x harmless-team-remove’
shrinks the rig: it drops a seat and every edge that touches it.  A seat
that still holds work needs another seat to take that work.  Without one
the removal is refused: ‘team seat holds work’.  Naming the same seat is
refused: ‘team fallback is the same seat’.  The rig file stays when its
last seat is removed.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-adopt’ binds a session that is already running to
a seat.  It does not start that session.  The runtime is ‘harmless’,
‘claude’, ‘codex’, ‘pi’, or ‘terminal’.  A Harmless session is the id of
a session directory that already exists, including one started by hand.
Claude Code, Codex, and Pi each take an absolute path to a file or
directory that already exists.  A terminal takes that path, or the id of
a live process such as a dev server or a log.  Replacing the binding
does not stop the session that was there.  ‘M-x harmless-team-release’
clears the binding.  It does not delete the session, the file, or the
process.  The name, the role, and the inbound work stay.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-send’ types text onto one seat, a comma-separated
list of seats, a pod, the rig name, or ‘*’.  The text is appended to
that seat's screen, and the send is refused when the text is not there
afterward.  It does not start a turn.  A Harmless seat, including one
filled by ‘M-x harmless-team-occupy’, gets one user line in
‘messages.jsonl’.  A terminal log is the screen and is appended.  Claude
Code, Codex, and Pi use ‘screen.txt’ in the session directory.  A live
process id is not typed into: ‘team seat has no screen’.  A directory
with no ‘screen.txt’ is refused the same way.

   The whole send is refused before any screen changes when a seat is
empty (‘team seat is not occupied’), at a prompt (‘team seat is at a
prompt’), or at a permission question (‘team seat is at a permission
question’).  A prompt is a last line ‘>’ or a last line that ends in
‘>’.  ‘Allow this tool?’ is a permission question.  A Harmless seat uses
its status instead of the message file.  ‘streaming’ is a prompt and
‘waiting-permission’ is a permission question.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-capture’ reads the screen.  A process id has no
file, so the capture is the transcript.  ‘M-x harmless-team-transcript’
reads every text sent to that seat, including text sent to an earlier
occupant.  Releasing the seat, or removing it, does not delete that
history.

   A blank send is refused: ‘team send is empty’.  A send that is not
text is refused: ‘team send must be a string’.  A target that names
nothing is refused: ‘team send is not a target’.  A missing seat is
refused: ‘team seat is missing’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-room-say’ appends one line to the rig's chatroom.
The line records the topic, the speaker, the text, and the time.  A
topic is letters and digits.  The speaker is any text.  The room is kept
in the rig file.  It does not start a turn.  ‘M-x
harmless-team-room-history’ reads the lines, oldest first.  With no
topic, it reads every topic.  ‘M-x harmless-team-room-wait’ blocks until
someone speaks in that topic.  Lines already stored do not count.  The
wait stops when nobody has spoken within
‘harmless-team-room-wait-seconds’ (10 seconds): ‘team room is still
quiet’.

   A blank topic is refused: ‘team room topic is empty’.  A topic that
is not text is refused: ‘team room topic must be a string’.  Any other
topic is refused: ‘team room topic is not a name’.  A speaker uses the
empty and must-be-a-string sentences, with ‘speaker’ in place of
‘topic’.  The text uses them too, with ‘text’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-pace’ starts a named sequence aimed at one seat.
The seat is ‘pod.member’ or the address.  ‘M-x harmless-team-pace-add’
appends one piece.  ‘M-x harmless-team-pace-show’ reads the sequence and
does not send.  ‘M-x harmless-team-pace-step’ sends the next piece to
that seat, then moves forward.  One call sends one piece.  There is no
command that sends the rest at once.  A seat at a prompt or a permission
question refuses the step, and the sequence stays on that piece.  A
finished sequence is refused: ‘team pace is finished’.

   A blank pace name is refused: ‘team pace is empty’.  A name that is
not text is refused: ‘team pace must be a string’.  Any other name is
refused: ‘team pace is not a name’.  Piece text uses the empty and
must-be-a-string sentences, with ‘text’ in place of ‘pace’.  The same
name again is refused: ‘team pace already exists’.  A missing pace is
refused: ‘team pace is missing’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-row’ opens one unit of owned work aimed at one
seat.  The seat is ‘pod.member’ or the address.  The body is the work.
The state is ‘open’.  The history starts with one line, ‘opened’.  The
row is kept in the rig file, so it stays when the seat's session is
replaced, released, or removed.  ‘M-x harmless-team-rows’ reads the
rows, oldest first.  With no seat, it reads every row.  ‘M-x
harmless-team-row-read’ reads one row by its id.  Reading by id does not
need the destination seat to still exist.

   A blank body is refused: ‘team row body is empty’.  A body that is
not text is refused: ‘team row body must be a string’.  A blank id is
refused: ‘team row is empty’.  An id that is not text is refused: ‘team
row must be a string’.  Any other id is refused: ‘team row is not an
id’.  A missing row is refused: ‘team row is missing’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-row-claim’ holds an open row.  The history gains
one line, ‘claimed’.  A held row is refused: ‘team row is held’.  A
closed row is refused: ‘team row is closed’.  ‘M-x
harmless-team-row-note’ appends one note to an open or held row.  A
closed row is refused: ‘team row is closed’.  A blank note is refused:
‘team row note is empty’.  A note that is not text is refused: ‘team row
note must be a string’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-row-close’ closes a held row.  An open row is
refused: ‘team row is not held’.  The reason is ‘handed_off_to’,
‘blocked_on’, ‘denied’, ‘canceled’, ‘no-follow-on’, or ‘escalation’.
The history gains one line, ‘delivered’, and the reason is stored.
Closing records delivery.  Acceptance stays the next seat's verdict, and
this command does not open another row.  A blank reason is refused:
‘team row reason is empty’.  A reason that is not text is refused: ‘team
row reason must be a string’.  Any other reason is refused: ‘team row
reason is not a reason’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-row-hand’ hands a held row to another seat.  The
source closes with reason ‘handed_off_to’ and the destination opens with
the same body.  Both changes are written together.  The destination
stays open, so acceptance remains that seat's own later claim.  An open
source is refused: ‘team row is not held’.  A closed source is refused:
‘team row is closed’.  The source's own seat is refused: ‘team row hand
is the same seat’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-row-park’ parks a held row on one wake.  A second
wake is refused: ‘team row is parked’.  An open row is refused: ‘team
row is not held’.  A blank wake is refused: ‘team row wake is empty’.  A
wake that is not text is refused: ‘team row wake must be a string’.  A
summary and a pointer to the evidence together ask a person.  The
summary is plain language.  The evidence is text, such as a path or a
note, and it is stored as text.  One without the other is refused: ‘team
row evidence is empty’ or ‘team row summary is empty’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-row-resolve’ records the decision in the history
and returns the row to held.  That is the owner's wake.  It does not
start a turn.  A row that is not parked is refused: ‘team row is not
parked’.  A blank decision is refused: ‘team row decision is empty’.  A
decision that is not text is refused: ‘team row decision must be a
string’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-intake’ records an observation on the rig.  It has
no seat and no owner.  ‘M-x harmless-team-intakes’ reads them, oldest
first.  A blank observation is refused: ‘team intake is empty’.  An
observation that is not text is refused: ‘team intake must be a string’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-board’ reads one view of the rows.  ‘held’ is a
row in ‘held’ or ‘parked’.  ‘stalled’ is a held row with nothing after
its claim.  ‘waiting’ is a parked row that asks a person.  The view does
not write.  A blank view is refused: ‘team board is empty’.  A view that
is not text is refused: ‘team board must be a string’.  Any other view
is refused: ‘team board is not a view’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-workflow’ declares an ordered list of steps and
opens the first one.  ‘M-x harmless-team-workflows’ reads them, oldest
first.  Closing the current step opens the next step's row in the same
write.  The owner decides that the step is done.  The reason records
delivery of this step.  The next row stays open.  The last close opens
no row.  Handing the current step is refused: ‘team row is a workflow
step’.  A blank name is refused: ‘team workflow is empty’.  A name that
is not text is refused: ‘team workflow must be a string’.  Any other
name is refused: ‘team workflow is not a name’.  No steps is refused:
‘team workflow has no steps’.  Steps that are not a list are refused:
‘team workflow steps must be a list’.  A step without a seat and a body
is refused: ‘team workflow step is not a step’.  A blank body is
refused: ‘team workflow body is empty’.  A body that is not text is
refused: ‘team workflow body must be a string’.  A workflow that already
exists is refused: ‘team workflow already exists’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-watchdog’ records one job on the rig: a reminder,
a pool of artifacts, a nudge to a workflow, an idle queue, or a seat
near its context wall.  ‘M-x harmless-team-watchdogs’ reads them, oldest
first.  ‘M-x harmless-team-watchdog-check’ reads the jobs that are due.
The job stays in the rig file, so it is still there after Emacs
restarts.  The check does not arm a timer, a schedule, or a monitor, and
it does not start a turn.  A reminder is due when its time has arrived.
An artifact pool is stored text and is not opened.  A nudge is due while
that workflow still has a row.  An idle job is due while its seat has an
open row.  A wall is due when the seat's last prompt reaches the
recorded percent of the known context window.  A blank kind is refused:
‘team watchdog is empty’.  A kind that is not text is refused: ‘team
watchdog must be a string’.  Any other kind is refused: ‘team watchdog
is not a kind’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-snapshot’ stores a named copy of the rig in its
own file.  ‘M-x harmless-team-snapshot-check’ reads that copy and
reports each seat: ‘resumed’, ‘rebuilt’, ‘fresh’, ‘waiting’, or
‘failed’.  The check does not write.  A fresh start is a list of seats,
or one seat, and the report labels it ‘fresh’.  ‘M-x
harmless-team-snapshot-restore’ writes that copy back only when no seat
failed.  A failed seat is refused: ‘team snapshot cannot restore’.  The
rig file is left unchanged.  A blank name is refused: ‘team snapshot is
empty’.  A name that is not text is refused: ‘team snapshot must be a
string’.  Any other name is refused: ‘team snapshot is not a name’.  A
snapshot that already exists is refused: ‘team snapshot already exists’.
A missing snapshot is refused: ‘team snapshot is missing’.  A fresh
value that is not a list is refused: ‘team snapshot fresh must be a
list’.  A seat that is not in the snapshot is refused: ‘team seat is
missing’.  A snapshot file that cannot be read is refused: ‘team
snapshot is not valid’.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-successor’ hands one seat to a session that is
already running.  The seat keeps its name, its edges, and its inbound
work.  The recap of decisions is written at that boundary.  ‘M-x
harmless-team-boundaries’ reads those recaps, oldest first.  The earlier
session keeps running.  A blank recap is refused: ‘team recap is empty’.
A recap that is not text is refused: ‘team recap must be a string’.  An
empty seat is refused: ‘team seat is not occupied’.  The same session
again is refused: ‘team successor is the same session’.  Nothing is
written when the successor cannot be bound.

