22 Health
*********

‘M-x harmless-team-health’ reads the rig and the files it names.  It
writes nothing and starts nothing.  Each seat is ‘empty’, ‘live’,
‘missing’, or ‘disagree’.  A service is ‘up’ or ‘down’.  Services are
listed beside the seats.  A service is not a seat.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-service’ records a name and a reference.  The
reference is a process id or an absolute path.  A missing path is not
created.  A process is not started.  ‘M-x harmless-team-service-remove’
forgets the name and leaves the path and the process in place.

   ‘M-x harmless-team-recover’ rewrites one seat when the live session
and the record disagree.  A missing session is cleared.  The seat keeps
its work and its profile.  A Harmless summary that names another real
session is retargeted, and the old directory stays.  An empty seat and a
live seat are refused: ‘team health agrees’.  A disagreement this
command cannot see through is refused: ‘team health cannot recover’.
That refusal writes nothing.

   Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘service name is empty’.  A
value that is not text is refused: ‘service name must be a string’.  A
path, ‘~’, and the filesystem root are refused: ‘service name is not a
name’.  A second service is refused: ‘service is already registered: ’,
followed by the name.  A missing service is refused: ‘no service named
’, followed by the name.

   Nil, ‘""’, and whitespace are refused: ‘service ref is empty’.  A
value that is not text is refused: ‘service ref must be a string’.  A
relative path, including ‘~’, is refused and is not expanded: ‘service
ref must be absolute’.  ‘/’ and a drive root are refused: ‘service ref
is the filesystem root’.  Zero is refused: ‘service ref is not a ref’.

   A broken rig is refused: ‘team rig is not valid’.  That file is left
unchanged.


